In October 1943, Bose announced a Provisional Government of Free India, the Azad Hind government. It had its own flag, stamps and money, and a few countries allied with Japan recognised it. At the end of that year he raised the Indian flag in Port Blair, in the [[@andaman|Andaman Islands]], which Japan had captured.

Bose gave the army stirring slogans. "Jai Hind" became its greeting and is still used today. The INA's marching song, "Kadam Kadam Badhaye Ja", is still played by Indian army bands. He also formed the Rani of Jhansi Regiment for women, led by a young doctor, Lakshmi Swaminathan, later known as Captain Lakshmi Sahgal.

{{story:world-war-two}}

In 1274 and 1281, the [[@mongols|Mongol]] rulers of China sent huge fleets to invade Japan. Samurai fought them on the beaches of Kyushu.

Both times, storms wrecked many Mongol ships. Japanese people called these storms *kamikaze*, "divine winds". The battles also showed that Mongol tactics, with group attacks and exploding bombs, were very different from samurai styles of fighting.

{{story:haiku}}

Historians estimate that around 100,000 skilled craftsmen and perhaps a million labourers worked on the palace. The finest timber came from forests in south-west China, thousands of kilometres away. Huge logs were floated down rivers and could take years to arrive. Special bricks were fired in [[kiln|kilns]] near Suzhou, and they were so smooth they became known as "golden bricks".

Some stone carvings weighed hundreds of tonnes. Records describe workers in winter pouring water on roads so it froze, then dragging the stones on sledges over the ice.

{{story:zheng-he}}

Indian scripts share a clever design. Each consonant letter carries a built-in "a" sound, so क is read "ka". To change the vowel, you add a small mark called a *matra*: कि (ki), कु (ku), के (ke). Two consonants can join into one combined letter, such as क्ष (ksha). Scripts that work this way are called [[abugida|abugidas]].

They also [[@words-alphabet-order|share an order]]. Instead of A, B, C, letters are grouped by where the sound is made in the mouth. They start at the throat (ka, kha, ga), then move to the palate, the roof of the mouth and the teeth, and finish at the lips (pa, pha, ba, ma). Say them aloud and you can feel the sounds travelling forward. This order comes from ancient Indian scholars who studied speech sounds with great care.

{{story:indus-cities}}

पौधे चल तो नहीं सकते, पर हिलते-डुलते हर समय हैं, बस बहुत धीरे-धीरे। जड़ें गुरुत्वाकर्षण के पीछे-पीछे नीचे बढ़ती हैं। अंकुर उसके खिलाफ़ ऊपर बढ़ते हैं। सूरजमुखी अपना सिर घुमाते हैं, और बेलें खंभों से लिपट जाती हैं।

{{story:plants-move}}

पौधे गुरुत्वाकर्षण को [[starch|स्टार्च]] के नन्हे दानों की मदद से भाँपते हैं। ये दाने खास [[cell|कोशिकाओं]] की तली में बैठ जाते हैं, जैसे बरनी में रेत। फिर एक [[hormone|हॉर्मोन]] एक तरफ़ जमा होने लगता है और उधर की कोशिकाओं को लंबा कर देता है। यह वही खिंचाव है जिसके बारे में तुम [[@gravity-fall|चीज़ें नीचे क्यों गिरती हैं]] में पढ़ सकते हो।
